## 2. Typical Exam Question: "How is an explosive adapted to exploit or break hard materials?"
When explaining how explosives break hard rock in engineering, the answer focuses on thermodynamics and wave mechanics:

* 
* Chemical Transition: The reaction instantly transforms a dense solid or liquid chemical into highly compressed, high-temperature gases (primarily nitrogen, water vapor, and carbon oxides).
* Shockwave Generation (Detonation): The reaction moves through the explosive material faster than the speed of sound, creating a supersonic shockwave that shatters the immediate surrounding rock (compressive stress).
* Gas Expansion (Heaving Effect): The trapped, rapidly expanding gases exert immense pressure against the walls of the blast hole. When this pressure exceeds the tensile strength of the rock, it forces the cracks to open wide, displacing and breaking the stone apart.
